    "summary": "Model built and PASSED all L1/L2/L3 checks.\n\nDiagnosis: The model-check failure was traced to the deck-lane decisions going out of sync with the instance. Re-writing the instance withdrew the derived decisions lane.xi and lane.mu and cleared the modal fact f1, leaving L2-2 (\"every span has a lane Pk and the impact factor agrees with the model's first vertical frequency\") unsatisfiable. I re-accepted lane.xi (=1.0, two design lanes, JTGD60-4.3.1 Table 4.3.1-5), reran the modal analysis to restore f1, and re-accepted lane.mu = 0.2006 from JTG D60-2015 4.3.2.\n\nModel summary (single simply supported span, spine_beam_2d):\n- Span 30 m, deck 10 m wide, carriageway 9 m, two-way, 2 design lanes, Highway Class II.\n- 3 precast prestressed small-box girders, C40; merged single girder: A = 3.417 m², I = 1.0648 m⁴, Ec = 32500 MPa, γ = 26 kN/m³, w = 88.84 kN/m, m = 9.056 t/m (girder self-weight only).\n- Two abutment support lines, each with 6 plain laminated elastomeric bearings (2 per girder end; 300×400 mm, 5×8 mm layers): S = 10.714, Kv = 8.518e6 kN/m, Kh = 18000 kN/m; vertical/longitudinal springs, rotation free.\n- Lane load: qk = 7.875 kN/m, Pk = 240 kN, ξ = 1.0, μ = 0.2006 (f1 = 3.40 Hz).\n\nKey results: f1 = 3.40 Hz; total self-weight reactions 2×1332.55 = 2665.09 kN (= 88.836 × 30); self-weight midspan moment 9994 kN·m; lane-load max moment 6449.6 kN·m."
